Light	  responsive	  metal-­‐organic	  frameworks	  as	  a	  controllable	  COreleasing	  cell	  culture	  substrate
A	   new	   carbon	   monoxide	   (CO)	   releasing	   material	   has	   been	   developed	   by	   embedding	   a	   manganese	   carbonyl	   complex, MnBr(bpydc) $(\mathsf{C O})_{3}$ (bpydc $\mathbf{\Sigma}=5{,}5{'}$ ′-­‐dicarboxylate- $^{2,2^{\prime}}$ -­‐bipyridine)	   into	   a	   highly	   robust	   Zr(IV)-­‐based	   metal-­‐organic	   framework (MOF).	   Efficient	   and	   controllable	   CO-­‐release	   was	   achieved	   under	   exposure	   to	   low	   intensity	   visible	   light.	   Size	   controllable nanocrystals	  of	  the	  photoactive	  MOF	  were	  obtained	  and	  their	  CO	  releasing	  properties	  were	  correlated	  with	  their	  crystal sizes.	   The	   photoactive	   crystals	   were	   processed	   into	   cellular	   substrates	   with	   biocompatible	   polymer	   matrix	   and	   light-­‐ induced	   delivery	   of	   CO	   and	   its	   subsequent	   cellular	   uptake	   was	   monitored	   using	   a	   fluorescent	   CO-­‐probe.	   The	   results discussed	  here	  demonstrate	  a	  new	  opportunity	  to	  use	  MOFs	  as	  macromolecular	  scaffolds	  towards	  CO-­‐releasing	  materials and	  an	  advantage	  of	  MOFs	  for	  high	  CO	  payloads	  essential	  in	  future	  therapeutic	  applications.
## Introduction
Carbon	  monoxide	  (CO)	  has	  not	  only	  been	  known	  for	  its	  toxicity, originating	  from	  an	  extraordinary	  affinity	  for	  the	  haemoglobin heme	   iron,	   but	   it	   is	   also	   an	   important	   cell	   signaling	   mediator, classified	   as	   a	   gasotransmitter,	   akin	   to	   nitric	   oxide	   and hydrogen	   sulfide.2,3	   In	   addition,	   CO	   is	   known	   to	   play	   a protective	   role	   in	   the	   tissue	   and	   organs	   through	   its	   anti-­‐ inflammation,	   anti-­‐apoptotic,	   and	   anti-­‐proliferative properties.4	  Thus,	  CO	  is	  postulated	  as	  an	  alternative	  drug	  to	  be employed	   when	   inflammation	   plays	   a	   damaging	   role	   such	   as respiratory	   and	   intestinal	   inflammation.5	   However,	   two	   main challenges	   have	   to	   be	   addressed	   before	   considering	   CO	   as	   a credible	   therapeutic	   agent;	   the	   handling	   issues	   arising	   from the	   gaseous	   state	   of	   CO	   and	   its	   systemic	   toxicity,	   and	   the control	   on	   the	   location,	   dosage	   and	   timing	   of	   the	   CO	   delivery. In	   this	   context,	   stable	   compounds	   that	   are	   able	   to	   store	   CO	   in the	   solid	   state	   (or	   in	   solution)	   and	   liberate	   it	   upon	   external stimuli,	  are	  of	  particular	  interest.	  Indeed,	  light	  is	  a	  non-­‐invasive stimulus,	   which	   can	   be	   manipulated	   in	   terms	   of	   intensity, wavelength	   and	   location.	   As	   such,	   photoactive	   CO-­‐releasing molecules	   (photo-­‐CORMs)	   mostly	   derived	   from	   carbonyl complexes,6-­‐8	   have	   been	   extensively	   studied	   and	   have	   shown their	   potential	   as	   intracellular	   CO	   delivery	   agents.9	   However,
molecular	   photoCORMs	   are	   difficult	   to	   localize	   due	   to	   their fast	   diffusion	   after	   administration,10	   which	   may	   cause	   toxicity to	   untargeted	   healthy	   tissues	   either	   by	   the	   action	   of	   the liberated	   CO	   or	   by	   the	   release	   of	   metal	   coligand	   fragments after	  the	  photoreaction.	  Therefore,	  the	  localized	  CO	  release	  in tissues	  or	  organs	  is	  still	  challenging.11
The	   hybridization	   of	   molecular	   photoCORMs	   with macromolecular	   or	   inorganic	   scaffolds	   is	   a	   promising	   strategy to synthesize photoinduced	   CO-­‐releasing materials (photoCORMAs)	   that	   are	   easier	   to	   localize,	   which	   facilitates	   a tissue-­‐specific	   therapy.12	   In	   addition,	   the	   payload	   of	   CO	   in photoCORMAs	   is	   higher	   than	   in	   their	   molecular	   counterparts, and	   the	   extent	   of	   leached	   by-­‐products	   is	   limited	   because	   the metal	   carbonyl	   moiety	   is	   immobilized	   in	   the	   scaffold.	   Thus,	   to date,	   photoCORMs	   have	   been	   assembled	   into	   dendritic structures,13 covalently	   immobilized	   onto	   nanoparticles surface,14-­‐16 protein	   cages,17 or	   organic	   polymers, 18 trapped into	  polymer	  fibers,19	  and	  mesoporous	  silica.20
Aside	   from	   other	   macromolecular	   scaffolds,	   metal-­‐organic frameworks	   (MOFs)	   constitute	   a	   distinct	   class	   of	   hybrid materials,	   assembled	   from	   metal	   ions	   or	   clusters	   and	   organic ligands.	   Their	   framework	   topology,	   pore	   size,	   pore	   shape	   and functionality	   can	   be	   modulated	   by	   a	   judicious	   choice	   of	   the molecular	   components.21,22	   This	   modularity	   in	   MOF	   synthesis makes	  this	  unique	  class	  of	  material	  promising	  for	  a	  wide	  range of	   applications	   in	   gas	   storage,	   molecular	   separation,	   catalysis, sensing	   and	   drug	   delivery.23	   Of	   particular	   interest	   is	   the possibility	   to	   incorporate	   photodonor	   ligands	   as	   part	   of framework	   scaffold,	   which	   helps	   to	   maximize	   the	   lightharvesting	   efficiency	   of	   photoCORMs	   by	   concentrating	   the photoactive	  moieties	  within	  a	  restricted	  space.	  In	  addition,	  the high	   surface	   area	   of	   MOFs	   ensures	   high	   payloads	   of photoactive	   species	   within	   the	   materials.	   Indeed,	   we	   have recently	  demonstrated	  the	  advantage	  of	  photoactive	  MOFs	  for controlled	   delivery	   of	   biologically	   active	   nitric	   oxide	   (NO) molecules	   at	   cellular	   level.24,25	   Although	   there	   has	   been	   only one	   example	   of	   CO-­‐releasing	   MOF,26	   in	   which	   CO	   gas	   is	   first adsorbed	  onto	  open	  metal	  sites	  and	  then	  it	  is	  steadily	  released through	   ligand	   exchange	   reaction	   with	   water	   in	   physiological media,	   to	   the	   best	   of	   our	   knowledge,	   highly	   controllable release	  of	  CO	  from	  MOFs	  is	  yet	  to	  be	  achieved.
Herein,	  we	  immobilized	  a	  photoactive	  manganese	  carbonyl complex	   within	   a	   robust	   zirconium-­‐based	   MOF	   and demonstrated	   efficient	   light-­‐induced	   CO-­‐release	   therefrom. Using	  the	  coordination	  modulation	  method,27,28	  we	  controlled the	   MOF	   crystal	   sizes,	   with	   which	   the	   CO-­‐donor	   loading	   and the	   photoactive	   efficiency	   were	   correlated.	   Furthermore,	   the immobilization	   of	   photoactive	   MOF	   particles	   into	   a	   polymer matrix	  serving	  as	  cell-­‐growth	  substrate	  allowed	  for	  observation of	   intracellular	   uptake	   of	   CO	   upon	   visible	   light	   irradiation	   on the	  substrate.
## Experimental	  section
## Materials	  and	  instrumentation
All	   reagents	   and	   reactants	   were	   purchased	   to	   Wako	   Pure Chemical	   Industries	   and	   used	   without	   further	   purification. PXRD	   measurements	   were	   performed	   with	   a	   Rigaku	   Smartlab (Dtex	   Ultra	   detector)	   operating	   with	   a	   rotating	   anode	   Cu	   Kα	   X-­‐ray generator $(\lambda=1.54\mathrm{~\AA})$ with	   a $40~\mathsf{k V}$ beam	   voltage	   and $200~\mathsf{m A}$ current.	   Thermogravimetric	   analyses	   (TGA)	   were	   carried	   out from	   room	   temperature	   to $750^{\circ}\mathsf C$ at $10\ ^{\circ}\mathsf{C\min^{-1}}$ heating	   rate with	  a	  Rigaku	  Thermo	  plus	  EVO2	  under	  a	  nitrogen	  atmosphere. The	   sorption	   isotherms	   of ${\sf N}_{2}$ a t $77~\mathsf{K}$ were	   recorded	   on	   a BELSORP-­‐max	   volumetric-­‐adsorption	   instrument	   from	   BEL Japan,	   Inc.	   The	   samples	   were	   dried	   at $50~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ under	   vacuum conditions	  for	  over $12\mathsf{h}$ before	  all	  measurements.	  Infrared	  (IR) spectroscopy	   data	   were	   recorded	   by	   using	   Jasco	   FT/IR-­‐6100 equipped	   with	   KBr	   pellets	   with $\mathsf{~\boldmath~1~}\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ resolution	   and accumulation	  of	  64	  scans.	  Observation	  was	  performed	  using	  a field-­‐emission	   scanning	   electron	   microscope	   with	   a	   JEOL Model	  JSM-­‐7001F4	  system	  operating	  at $5\mathsf{k V}$ and $5\upmu\mathsf{A}$ current.
## Synthetic	  procedures
Synthesis	   of	   UiO-­‐67-­‐bpy.	   The	   ligand $^{2,2^{\prime}}$ -­‐bipyridine- $^{5,5^{\prime}}$ -­‐ dycarboxylic	   acid	   (bpydc)	   was	   synthesized	   according	   to reported	   procedures.29	   DMF	   solution	   containing	   bpydc	   (20 $\mathsf{m}\mathsf{M})$ and $Z r C l_{4}$ $(20~\mathsf{m M})$ and	   acetic	   acid	   (30	   or	   90	   eqv)	   was prepared	  and	  transferred	  to	  a	  teflon	  vessel	  which	  was	  heated at $120^{\circ}{\mathsf{C}}$ for	  24	  hours.	  The	  resulting	  white	  microcrystals	  were recovered	  by	  centrifugation	  and	  washed	  with	  DMF	  three	  times. Finally,	  the	  crystals	  were	  immersed	  in	  THF	  and	  the	  solvent	  was exchanged	   once	   very	   day	   for	   three	   days.	   Samples	   were	   dried under	  vacuum	  before	  loading	  and	  sorption	  experiments.
Loading	   of	   UiO-­‐67-­‐bpy.	   Microcrystals	   of	   UiO-­‐67-­‐bpy	   (22 mg, $0.01\mathrm{\mmol}$ )	   were	   suspended	   in	   a	   mixture	   of	   THF:Toluene (1:1, $10~\mathsf{m l}$ )	  containing $\mathsf{M n B r}(\mathsf C\mathsf{O})_{5}$ $(25.4~\mathrm{mg},0.093~\mathrm{mmol})$ and stirred	  at	  room	  temperature	  for $6\mathfrak{h}$ .	  Subsequently,	  the	  mixture was	   heated	   at $90~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for	   2	   hours.	   The	   orange	   microcrystals were	  recovered	  by	  centrifugation	  and	  thoroughly	  washed	  with THF	   and	   dried	   under	   vacuum.	   Samples	   were	   kept	   in	   the	   dark under	  argon	  atmosphere.
Synthesis	   of	   MnBr(dmbpy) $(C O)_{3}$ .	   The	   synthesis	   was adapted	  from	  reported	  procedures.30	  In	  a	  round	  bottom	  flask, $\mathsf{M n B r}(\mathsf C\mathsf{O})_{5}$ $(40.7~\mathrm{~mg},~0.148~\mathrm{~mmol}s)$ and $^{5,5^{\prime}}$ -­‐dimethyl- $^{2,2^{\prime}}$ - bipyridine $(30~\mathrm{~mg},~0.163~\mathrm{~mmol}s)$ were	   solubilized	   in	   diethyl ether $(10~\mathsf{m l})$ ).	   The	   solution	   was	   refluxed	   at $40~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for	   3	   hours. The	  orange	  solid	  was	  recovered	  by	  filtration	  and	  washed	  with diethyl	   ether.	   Finally	   it	   was	   dried	   at	   room	   temperature	   under vacuum.
Loading	   efficiency determination.	   The extent of metalation	   on	   UiO-­‐67-­‐bpy	   was	   determined	   by X-­‐ray Fluorescence	   (XRF),	   transmission	   electron	   microscopy-­‐energy dispersive	  X-­‐ray	  spectroscopy	  (TEM-­‐EDX)	  and	  TGA.
CO	   detection.	   A	   suspension	   in	   THF	   of	   the	   sample	   to	   be analyzed	   was	   spin	   coated	   on	   a	   glass	   substrate.	   The	   weight	   of the	   sample	   was	   between	   50	   and $100~{\upmu\mathrm{g}}$ .	   The	   sample	   was placed	  in	  a	  custom-­‐made	  chamber	  with	  a	  glass	  window	  on	  the top	   and	   irradiated	   with	   a	   300-­‐W	   Xenon	   lamp	   (Asahi	   Spectra Max-­‐303	   equipped	   with	   a	   300-­‐	   to $600-\mathsf{n m}$ ultraviolet–visible module	  and $\textbf{\times}1.0$ collimator	   lens).	   The	   light	   intensity	   was	   0.38 $\mathsf{m w/c m}^{2}$ .	  The	  released	  CO	  gas	  from	  CORF-­‐1	  was	  carried	  to	  the CO	  detector	  (HALO	  3	  TM	  trace	  gas	  analyzer	  from	  Tiger	  Optics) by $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ flow $(500~\mathrm{ml/min}$ flow	  rate)	  (Figure	  S11).
Cell	   culture	   procedure.	   To	   prepare	   extracellular stimulation	   scaffold, $1-1.5~\mathsf{m g}$ of	   CORF-­‐1_Small	   79	   crystals and	   PDMS	   layer	   were	   deposited	   on $35~\mathsf{m m}$ glass	   bottom	   dish (Skylight	   glass	   base	   dish,	   IWAKI)	   by	   spin	   coating.	   The $500~{\upmu\mathsf{M}}$ solutions	  of	  COP-­‐1	  in	  DMSO	  was	  diluted	  by	  DPBS $(+C\mathsf{a},+M\mathsf{g})$ to $1.0\upmu\mathrm{M}$ .	  HeLa	  cells	  were	  cultured	  in	  prepared	  glass	  bottom	  dish, which	   is	   including	   PDMS/CORF-­‐1	   layer,	   with $2m L$ of	   cell culture	   medium	   (Dulbecco’s	   Modified	   Eagle	   Medium supplemented	  with $10\%$ fetal	   bovine	   serum).	   HeLa	   cells	   were incubated	  at $37^{\circ}\mathsf C$ in	  a	  humidified	  atmosphere	  of $5\%(\mathsf{v}/\mathsf{v})$ CO2 in	   air	   for	   1	   day.	   Before	   stimulation,	   cells	   were	   incubated	   with $1.5~\mathsf{m L}$ of $1.0~\upmu\mathsf{M}$ COP-­‐1	   solution	   for $^\textrm{\scriptsize1h}$ to	   introduce	   COP-­‐1 into	  cells.	  Afterward,	  COP-­‐1	  solution	  was	  replaced	  with	  DMEM and	  the	  incubated	  for $^{1\mathfrak{h}}$ at $37^{\circ}\mathsf C$ .
Confocal	   fluorescence	   imaging.	   The	   illumination	   of	   the MOF-­‐based	   substrate	   was	   carried	   out	   by	   white	   light	   (light power	  150	  W)	  for	  90	  seconds	  and	  then	  incubated	  for $10\mathrm{\min}$ at $37^{\circ}\mathsf C$ in $5\%\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ before	  imaging.	  All	  fluorescent	  images	  used	  for cell	   imaging	   were	   acquired	   on	   Olympus	   FV-­‐1000.	   The	   imaging parameters	   for	   the	   COP-­‐1	   were	   excitation	   at $488\ n m$ and emission	  at $490\mathrm{-}630\mathrm{nm}$ .
## Results	  and	  discussion
## Size	  controlled	  synthesis	  of	  CORF-­‐1	  crystals
Inspired	   by	   previous	   reports	   on	   photoactive	   manganese carbonyl	   complexes,	   we	   sought	   to	   address	   the	   CO-­‐releasing properties	   of	   the $\mathsf{M n}(\mathsf{C O})_{3}$ moieties	   when	   these	   are coordinatively	  immobilized	  in	  a	  MOF	  structure.	  Our	  strategy	  to design	   the	   CO-­‐releasing	   framework	   involves	   the	   zirconium-­‐ based	   UiO-­‐67-­‐type	   framework,31 $Z\Gamma_{6}{\sf O}_{4}({\sf O H})_{4}({\sf b p v d c})_{6},$ (UiO-­‐67-­‐ bpy,	   bpydc $\mathit{\Theta}=\ 5,5^{\prime}$ ′-­‐dicarboxylate- $^{\cdot2,2^{\prime}}$ ′-­‐bipyridine)	   as	   starting material	   and	   the	   subsequent	   metalation	   of	   its	   open $^{2,2^{\prime}\mathrm{.}}$ - bipyridine	   (bpy)	   coordination	   centers	   to	   form	   the	   photoactive $\mathsf{M n B r}(\mathsf{b p y})(\mathsf{C O})_{3}$ core	   on	   the	   MOF	   walls,	   leading	   to MnBr(bpydc) $(\mathsf{C O})_{3}@$ UiO-­‐67	   (CORF-­‐1:	   CORF $\mathbf{\sigma}=\mathbf{\sigma}$ CO-­‐releasing framework)	  (Figure	  1).	  The	  straightforward	  formation	  of	  CORF-­‐ 1	  using	  MnBr(bpydc) $(\mathsf{C O})_{3}$ as	  metalloligand	  was	  not	  successful, due	   to	   the	   decomposition	   of	   the	   manganese	   complex	   at elevated	   temperature $(120~^{\circ}\mathsf{C})$ .31	   Note	   that	   the	   similar	   Mn-­‐ containing	   framework	   based	   on	   UiO-­‐67-­‐bpy	   with	   the	   mixed composition	   (1/1)	   between	   bpydc	   and	   bpdc	   (biphenyl-­‐4,4- dicarboxylic	   acid)	   was	   previously	   reported;32	   however,	   the mixed	   ligand	   framework	   limits	   the	   extent	   of	   metalation.	   Here we	   use	   only	   bpydc	   for	   the	   synthesis	   of	   CORF-­‐1	   and	   try	   to achieve	  the	  very	  high	  CO	  loading	  capacity.
First	  we	  optimized	  the	  synthesis	  conditions	  to	  afford	  highly crystalline	   UiO-­‐67-­‐bpy	   with	   tunable	   sizes	   (Figure	   S1).	   The bpydc	  ligand $(20~\mathsf{m M})$ and $Z r C l_{4}$ $(20~\mathsf{m M})$ were	  reacted	  in	  DMF at $\boldsymbol{120^{\circ}C}$ in	  the	  presence	  of	  30	  or	  90	  equivalents	  of	  acetic	  acid (HAc)	   as	   modulator.	   As	   expected,	   the	   amount	   of	   HAc	   in	   the reaction	  had	  a	  significant	  impact	  on	  the	  crystal	  size	  of	  UiO-­‐67- bpy;	  when	  30	  eqv	  of	  HAc	  were	  used,	  the	  crystals	  obtained	  had a	   mean	   size	   of $260\ \mathrm{nm}\pm80\ \mathrm{nm}$ whereas	   the	   mean	   size	   of	   the crystals	  increased	  up	  to $1200\ \mathsf{n m}\pm180\ \mathsf{n r}$ m	  with	  increasing	  the amount	  of	  modulator	  to	  90	  eqv	  (Figure	  2).
After	   washing	   and	   activating	   the	   UiO-­‐67-­‐bpy	   crystals,	   the post-­‐synthetic	   metalation	   of $\mathsf{M n B r}(\mathsf C\mathsf{O})_{5}$ was	   carried	   out. Initially,	   the	   crystals	   were	   immersed	   in	   a	   THF/toluene	   (1:1) solution	  of $\mathsf{M n B r}(\mathsf C\mathsf{O})_{5}$ and	  heated	  at $90^{\circ}C$ for	  2h	  as	  previously described.33	  Even	  though	  this	  process	  afforded	  the	  metalation of	   bpydc,	   its	   yield	   was	   poor $(40\%)$ as	   determined	   by	   X-­‐ray fluorescence	   (XRF)	   experiments	   and	   transmission	   electron microscopyenergy	   dispersive $\mathsf{x}$ -­‐ray	   spectroscopy	   (TEM-­‐EDX) (Table	   S1).	   In	   order	   to	   increase	   the	   loading	   amount,	   we implemented	   a	   two-­‐step	   loading	   process;	   first	   the	   precursor solution	  containing $\mathsf{M n B r}(\mathsf{C O})_{5}$ was	   allowed	   to	   diffuse	   into	   the crystals	   for $6\ h$ at	   RT	   and	   subsequently	   the	   temperature	   was raised	   to $90^{\circ}C$ for	   2h	   to	   promote	   the	   coordination	   reaction with	   bpy	   inside	   the	   MOF.	   This	   strategy	   allowed	   significantly higher	   yields	   of	   loading	   (Table	   S1,	   Figure	   S2).	   The	   extent	   of loading	   was	   also	   largely	   affected	   by	   crystal	   size;	   the	   smaller crystals	   had	   higher	   loading	   efficiency	   due	   to	   the	   shorter diffusion	   path.	   Thus,	   the	   loading	   of	   the	   smaller	   crystals reached $79\%$ (hereafter	   called	   CORF-­‐1_small_79)	   while	   in	   the case	   of	   the	   bigger	   crystals	   the	   loading	   was $60\%$ (CORF-­‐ 1_big_60).	   Interestingly,	   we	   observed	   that	   by	   repeating	   this process	   a	   second	   time	   on	   CORF-­‐1_small_79,	   almost	   fully loaded	   samples	   with $95\%$ loading	   efficiency	   (CORF-­‐ 1_small_95)	  were	  obtained.
As	   confirmed	   by	   powder	   X-­‐ray	   diffraction	   (PXRD) experiments	   and	   scanning	   electron	   microscope	   (SEM) observations,	   neither	   the	   crystallinity	   nor	   the	   morphology	   of the	  samples	  were	  altered	  by	  the	  loading	  process	  (Figure	  S3	  and S4).	   In	   addition,	   TEM-­‐EDX	   analysis	   of	   all	   CORF-­‐1	   samples revealed	  an	  homogeneous	  distribution	  of $\mathsf{M n}(!)$ ions	  within	  the structure	   (Figure	   S5).	   The	   nitrogen	   adsorption	   experiment performed	   at $77~\mathsf{K}$ indicated	   a	   decrease	   of	   BET	   surface	   area (BET)	  upon	  loading.	  The $S_{\mathsf{B E T}}$ of	  the	  smaller	  crystals	  of	  UiO-­‐67- bpy	   dropped	   from $2818\ \mathsf{m}^{2}/\mathsf{g}$ to $1077~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ and $586~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ for CORF-­‐1_small_79	   and	   CORF-­‐1_small_95,	   respectively	   (Figure 3).	   These	   results	   are	   consistent	   with	   the	   expected	   pore blocking	   caused	   by	   the $\mathsf{M n B r}(\mathsf C\mathsf{O})_{3}$ incorporation	   within	   the UiO-­‐67-­‐bpy	  structure.
The	   formation	   of	   the $\mathsf{M n B r}(\mathsf{b p y d c})(\mathsf{C O})_{3}$ moiety	   within CORF-­‐1	   was	   evidenced	   by	   FTIR	   spectroscopy	   with	   the appearance	   of	   the	   characteristic	   CO	   stretching	   vibrations	   at 2032	  and $1946~{\mathsf{c m}}^{-1}$ (Figure	  S6a).34	  Additionally,	  the	  solid-­‐state
